{"kind":"artifact","text":"gs query selectors at exact clean head 7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff on request/gs-query-selectors, four commits over main 6f60fe6c. Succeeds a1055e9d at cmd/gs, the current main-line artifact. This answers request 7d38d1c9 under promise d4ec8353. Companions at docs and internal/statusview stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.\n\nTHIS REPLACES MY OWN c132c5d6, WHICH NAMED A DEAD HEAD. That artifact stood at fd79bd7f, two heads back. An artifact naming a commit that is no longer the head cannot carry a review, which is why this lane could not advance. I retire it separately rather than resting on it: resting a new artifact on one I am about to supersede would propagate the retirement into the successor and leave it stale from birth. So succession here runs from the main-line artifact, and the dead-head trio is withdrawn by three bare supersessions naming these successors.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD NOW CONTAINS, against the ratified changes-requested verdict 4e546040 and the requester's ruling 56565ba9.\n\nTHE TYPE SPLIT, which is the ruling's central demand. statusview.ArtifactQuery — the type MCP decodes into and the resident's /v0/artifact-query accepts — is restored to exactly three fields, paths, limit and cursor. I diffed its declaration against main 6f60fe6c and it is identical. The CLI selectors live in a separate ArtifactSelection, read by BuildArtifactSelectionPage, which shares the filtering, rows, pagination and cursor implementation with the remote path. That satisfies condition 2, which asked for one shared query implementation, without the struct sharing that leaked in the first place — the distinction the requester drew, and it is the right one.\n\nI MUTATION-CHECKED THE LEAK GUARD, AND GOT IT WRONG TWICE BEFORE GETTING IT RIGHT. My first mutation re-added State and Reaches to ArtifactQuery and both guard tests stayed green. My second did the same and read the MCP boundary test. Green again. Neither was evidence of a weak test: the fields are dead there, because BuildArtifactPage constructs its selection with State: ArtifactStateLive and no Reaches, so nothing reads them. The mutation that actually reintroduces the defect is making BuildArtifactPage honour them, and under that one TestSelectiveToolsUseResidentSelectionWithoutFetchingStatus fails immediately: \"undeclared MCP selector map[state:retired] changed artifact semantics\", MatchingTotal 0 where the contract is 1. Restored, green. So the guard the ruling asked for exists and bites, and the split is structural rather than merely tested — a field re-added to the wire type does nothing until someone also wires it through the builder.\n\nTHE REVIEW GATE NO LONGER LOCKS ITSELF SHUT. I filed assert 747ab00e when the first cap arrived, because I measured 58 unique actionable approved heads against a hard ceiling of 50, which made ApprovedHeadsOmitted non-zero on every invocation, Quiet() false for ever and gs reviews permanently red — the unreachable precondition that notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md warns is the one failure an irreversible gate cannot have. The head now separates the bounded display from the complete decision: Quiet() is Awaiting == 0 again, ActionableApprovedHeads returns the whole deduplicated population, cmd/gs classifies that population rather than the capped sample, and the refusal keys on the Git classification totals. So a head that landed clears no matter how many historical approvals precede it, while any single response stays bounded. That is a better answer than the three repairs I suggested.\n\nTWO NEW COMMANDS close condition 1: gs supersession-plan for the retirement-act generation, and gs staleness-wave for the whole-log measurement. Both were absent from the head codex reviewed, and the requester confirmed their absence was condition 1 unmet rather than a judgement I was free to make.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT WRITE, said plainly. Another session held this worktree and implemented the repair. My own agent found it mid-flight, made no edits and stood down, because a head assembled from two interleaved implementations is unreviewable. I contributed the earlier commits, the ancestry batching, the assert that caught the cap defect, and this verification; I did not write the four commits at this head. I am publishing because the promise is mine and the requester ruled the commitment stays with me — 6cbe55d475 — and because nobody else can retire my artifacts. Every claim above I checked against the tree rather than against anyone's report.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head after the tree was clean: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok and no FAIL; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0 with no data race; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check exit 0; git status --porcelain empty. The head sits four commits over main 6f60fe6c and its two-dot and three-dot file lists agree.","body":{"commit":"7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff","path":"cmd/gs"}}